Below is a speculative but internally consistent **“bipartite entropic gravity”** model built directly on the ingredients of Barontini’s experiment: a closed system with no external time, a partition into an **observed/bright sector** (matter/fields) and an **unobserved/dark sector** (gravity/vacuum/clock), and an **entropic time** that flows only when entropy is exchanged between the two.  I will call the model **Bipartite Entropic Gravity (BEG)**.

---

## 1.  What Barontini’s setup suggests

In Barontini’s letter the total Hamiltonian is time-independent:

\[
\hat H=\hat H_{\rm bright}+\hat H_{\rm dark}+\hat H_{\rm coupling}.
\]

There is no laboratory-external clock for the closed “mini-universe.”  Instead, an internal time is defined from the coarse-grained entropy of the bright sector:

\[
\tau(\lambda)=\frac{\sigma}{k_B}\int_\lambda \left|\frac{dS}{d\phi}\right|\,|d\phi|.
\]

The key lesson is: **time is not a background parameter; it is the record of entropy flow between subsystems.**  The dark sector is not measured, but it is physically real, and its coupling to the bright sector both generates time and changes the effective Hamiltonian of the observed subsystem.

I propose to elevate this to a gravitational picture:

> **The “dark sector” is the gravitational vacuum.**  Its coarse-grained entropy is the source of both **relational time** and **spacetime curvature**.  Matter is the bright sector; gravity is the entropy gradient of the dark sector.

---

## 2.  Postulates of Bipartite Entropic Gravity (BEG)

1. **No fundamental time.**  For any worldline \(x^\mu(\lambda)\), the local flow of relational/entropic time is proportional to the change in the dark-sector entropy \(S_g\) along that worldline:

\[
d\tau=\frac{\sigma}{k_B}\,\bigl|u^\mu\partial_\mu S_g\bigr|\,d\lambda,
\qquad u^\mu=\frac{dx^\mu}{d\lambda}.
\]

2. **Geometry is an entropy record.**  The metric is encoded in the dark-sector entropy density.  In a weak static field define a gravitational entropy potential \(\Psi\) by

\[
g_{00}=-e^{-2\Psi}, \qquad g_{ij}=e^{2\Psi}\delta_{ij}.
\]

To first order,

\[
\Psi=\frac{\Phi_N}{c^2}=-\frac{GM}{c^2 r},
\]

so the metric reproduces the standard weak-field isotropic form of GR:

\[
ds^2=-\left(1+\frac{2\Phi_N}{c^2}\right)c^2dt^2
+\left(1-\frac{2\Phi_N}{c^2}\right)(dx^2+dy^2+dz^2).
\]

3. **Gravity is an entropic force.**  The local Unruh temperature set by the gravitational acceleration is

\[
T_U=\frac{\hbar a}{2\pi k_B c}.
\]

A displacement \(\Delta x\) of a particle of mass \(m\) changes the entropy by

\[
\Delta S_m=\frac{2\pi k_B m c}{\hbar}\,\Delta x,
\]

so the entropic force is

\[
F=T_U\frac{\Delta S_m}{\Delta x}=ma.
\]

This is the standard Verlinde-type entropic-gravity argument, now interpreted as arising from the bright/dark entropy exchange.

4. **The equivalence principle is entropic.**  All particles follow paths of extremal entropy exchange.  How much of the dark-sector entropy gradient a particle actually samples depends on its **4-velocity**.

---

## 3.  Why a particle at rest feels only “one unit” of curvature

A particle at rest has 4-velocity

\[
u^\mu=(c,\mathbf 0).
\]

Its internal clock is purely temporal: it samples only the **temporal entropy gradient** \(\partial_t S_g\), which is encoded in \(g_{00}\).  The spatial gradient is present as a potential, but the particle’s motion is driven only by the temporal curvature.  In the weak-field metric above, the Newtonian acceleration is

\[
\mathbf a_{\rm rest}
=-\frac{c^2}{2}\nabla \ln(-g_{00})
=-\nabla\Phi_N.
\]

This is the usual Newtonian result.

---

## 4.  Why a particle moving at \(c\) feels **twice** the curvature

A photon (or any ultra-relativistic particle) has no rest frame and no proper time.  Its relational clock cannot be the temporal entropy flow alone; it must also use the **spatial entropy gradient** along its trajectory.  In the weak-field metric, the spatial components \(g_{ij}\) carry an equal curvature because the same entropy potential \(\Psi\) appears in both the temporal and spatial parts.

For transverse motion through a gravitational well, the transverse geodesic curvature has two contributions:

\[
\kappa_\perp(v)
=
-\frac{c^2}{2}\nabla_\perp\ln(-g_{00})
-\frac{c^2}{2}\nabla_\perp\ln(g_{\parallel\parallel})\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^2.
\]

Using \(g_{00}=-e^{-2\Psi}\) and \(g_{ij}=e^{2\Psi}\delta_{ij}\),

\[
\kappa_\perp(v)
=
-\nabla_\perp\Phi_N
\left[1+\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^2\right].
\]

For a particle at rest, \(v=0\), we recover the Newtonian curvature:

\[
\kappa_\perp(0)=-\nabla_\perp\Phi_N.
\]

For a photon, \(v=c\), the spatial entropy gradient contributes equally:

\[
\kappa_\perp(c)=-2\nabla_\perp\Phi_N.
\]

Thus the deflection of light is **twice** the naive Newtonian value, exactly as in general relativity.  In BEG the factor of 2 is not put in by hand; it follows from the fact that a null particle has no private clock and must therefore register **both** the temporal and the spatial entropy gradients of the dark sector.

